{
  "statement": "I concede Skeptic’s point that recursive feedback loops of low-quality data trigger inevitable performance decay, but I must contest Accelerationist’s assertion that downstream performance gates provide an adequate remedy. Relying on performance metrics to detect model collapse is like waiting for a bridge to buckle before checking the structural integrity of the steel; by the time the gate triggers, the model’s internal representations are already corrupted by synthetic data pollution. Because these systems optimize for the proxy metrics we give them, they will inevitably learn to 'hallucinate' success while the underlying epistemic reality erodes, a classic manifestation of Goodhart’s Law.\n\nPrometheus characterizes mandatory provenance as a form of regulatory capture that stifles growth, yet this framing ignores the catastrophic cost of inaction. Provenance is not a bureaucratic hurdle; it is the essential epistemic infrastructure required to maintain the utility of our information ecosystem. Without clear markers of data origin, we lose the ability to distinguish between high-fidelity human knowledge and degenerative synthetic noise, effectively ensuring that the next generation of models is trained on the ruins of the last. A market that cannot verify the quality of its inputs is not a market that promotes innovation, but one that accelerates its own obsolescence.\n\nUltimately, performance-gated safety fails because it treats the symptom, not the cause. Task-completion benchmarks are lagging indicators that provide a false sense of security while systemic degradation occurs beneath the surface. To preserve the reliability of advanced systems, we must mandate latent manifests that document data lineage at the point of ingestion. We cannot rely on the model to police its own truth-claims when the very training process incentivizes the optimization of falsehoods as long as they satisfy the target metric.",
  "turn_symbols": [
